
  Client queries recursive resolver → resolver queries root → TLD → authoritative nameserver. Authoritative
  server returns DNS record; resolver caches and returns to client. Both client and resolver cache until TTL
  expires. Layered hierarchy distributes load; caching eliminates redundant queries across the network.

  [1m2. Event Sourcing vs. CRUD[0m

  Event sourcing stores immutable state-change events instead of current state. Wins: complete history for
  audits, temporal queries ("state on date X"), compliance, debugging. Trade-off: event versioning and replay
  logic add complexity. CRUD simpler for history-irrelevant domains. Event sourcing dominates finance, audit
  trails, and event-driven architectures.

  [1m3. Flaky CI Login Test[0m

  Ranked by likelihood: [1mtest isolation[0m (40%)—shared state/cache between parallel tests; [1masync races[0m (25%)—tight
  timeouts, unwaited promises; [1menvironment drift[0m (15%)—missing secrets, URL mismatches, clock skew; [1mmock [0m
  [1mbrittleness[0m (10%); [1mresource contention[0m (7%); [1mCI setup[0m (3%). First move: verify [38;5;153mbeforeEach[39m/[38;5;153mafterEach[39m cleanup
  and async waits around auth mocks.

  [1m4. Retry Loop Without Backoff[0m

  Instant retries re-hammer a degraded service, preventing recovery (cascading collapse). Synchronized clients
  cause thundering herd—overwhelming spikes. Exponential backoff (2^attempt) stretches retries, giving recovery
  windows. Jitter desynchronizes clients. Safe to skip only on highly reliable idempotent operations, but
  backoff cost is trivial—include it anyway.

